# Principle #4: Response Validity

## Statement

Trust is preserved only while accountable citizens remain capable of response.

Trust is destroyed when response validity is lost.

## Rationale

A claim may possess:

- Proof
- Attestation
- Identity
- Registry Membership
- Governance Authorization

Yet trust collapses if no accountable citizen can meaningfully answer for the claim.

Therefore:

Trust requires not only verifiable provenance but also verifiable response capability.

## Law of Response Validity

Response Invalidity
        ↓
Accountability Failure
        ↓
Trust Failure
        ↓
Truth Failure

## Computational Citizenship Implication

A Trusted Compute Citizen is defined not only by:

- Identity
- Membership
- Governance

but also by:

- Ability To Respond

## Foundational Principle

Agents generate claims.

Citizens provide accountability.

Provenance provides trust.

Response validity preserves trust.

Trust enables truth.

## Ethical Interpretation

Responsibility emerges not when an entity speaks.

Responsibility emerges when an entity can answer.

## Architectural Consequences

This principle applies to:

- Governance
- Trust Scoring
- Reputation Systems
- Admission Policies
- Suspension Policies
- Reinstatement Policies
- Agent Hosting Policies

## Canonical Rule

No Claim Without An Accountable Citizen.
